WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.600
Ethan Suplee made a name&nbsp;
for himself as the lovable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.480
beefy Louie in "Remember the Titans,"&nbsp;
but you wouldn't recognize him today…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:14.040
could a come to Jesus moment with a man&nbsp;
who once played Jesus be the reason why?

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.760
If you've been following Ethan Suplee's career,&nbsp;
you know much of it has been spent playing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:24.200
in his own words, "roles for fat kids." After&nbsp;
decades of being the butt of fat kid jokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.920
and searching for a weight-loss program that&nbsp;
worked, Suplee finally found one that stuck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.434
and helped him get down from his&nbsp;
heaviest weight of over 530 pounds.

00:00:33.434 --> 00:00:40.480
"I'm now 260. In between there, there&nbsp;
was weight loss and weight gain."

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.040
The actor has been showcasing his new physique on&nbsp;
Instagram of late, and fans who've been following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.680
him for decades are finding it hard to believe&nbsp;
the buff guy in the pictures is actually him.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.080
So impressive is Suplee's transformation,&nbsp;
the actor was showcased in Men's Health in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:00.480
a January 2020 interview. He spoke candidly&nbsp;
about his struggles to not just lose weight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.000
but keep it off amid a busy work&nbsp;
schedule that didn't allow for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.328
much time in the gym, saying -

00:01:06.328 --> 00:01:08.120
"I wasn't factoring in how I was 

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:13.440
going to maintain my weight at work when I was&nbsp;
working like, 14 hours a day, five days a week."

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.800
Counterintuitively, it was Suplee's&nbsp;
demanding work-life that actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.680
placed him in a position to make&nbsp;
healthy changes in the first place.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.040
While the beloved character actor is&nbsp;
famous for his scene-stealing turns in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:28.880
dramatic films like "American History X"&nbsp;
and zany sitcoms like former must-see TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.920
staple "My Name Is Earl," it's one&nbsp;
of Suplee's most under-appreciated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.080
performances that he actually credits&nbsp;
with introducing him to the gym.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.120
It seems his casting on Hulu's" Chance,"&nbsp;
which required some weight training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.480
combined with a revelatory TED&nbsp;
Talk about the science of food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.160
helped Suplee find the right&nbsp;
mix. He told Men's Health -

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.560
"The most important thing I would want&nbsp;
anyone to take away is that for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.400
the biggest change was understanding how food&nbsp;
works. And the more I feel that I understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.175
scientifically, the more power I have over it."

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.960
On "Chance," Suplee played fan-favorite&nbsp;
Darius "D" Pringle opposite Hugh Laurie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:11.840
titular neuropsychiatrist. On the show, which&nbsp;
only ran for two woefully under-watched seasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:18.160
between 2016 and 2017, Suplee debuted a notably&nbsp;
more toned physique than the one he made famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.240
in earlier appearances — though nothing&nbsp;
on par with the bod he's rocking today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:28.600
When "Chance" was canceled, Suplee must have&nbsp;
made a commitment to stick to his routine.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:32.960
At the same time Ethan Suplee was showing&nbsp;
off his reformed figure on "Chance," he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.640
also appearing in a recurring capacity&nbsp;
on Netflix's hit sitcom "The Ranch,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:42.360
where he played Colt and Rooster's high&nbsp;
school buddy, Billy "Beer Pong" Tonkins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:47.560
who just so happens to be Officer Tonkins&nbsp;
now. A fan-favorite guest star, Suplee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:54.320
shows up on 13 episodes across all four seasons,&nbsp;
reminding viewers of his genuine acting range.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.372
"You're using your holster to hold a beer."

00:02:56.372 --> 00:03:03.012
"Yeah. Pretty cool huh? Wait - where's my gun?"

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:08.000
After "Chance" was canceled, Suplee was&nbsp;
forced to find work elsewhere. In 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.960
he appeared as Bill Shaker on the "Twin&nbsp;
Peaks" revival season before playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:18.040
Tommy for six episodes in Season 3 of "Santa&nbsp;
Clarita Diet." From there, he played Gil on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:23.880
"Good Girls" in 2020 and even voiced Bob&nbsp;
the Bigfoot on the animated "Vampirina."

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.280
Unfortunately, those are the last recurring&nbsp;
television roles Suplee has had in the past few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:34.040
years. Sure, he's slated to appear in the Indian&nbsp;
comedy series "The Pradeeps of Pittsburgh" once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:39.760
the current SAG-AFTRA strike ends, but here's&nbsp;
hoping he finds his way back to a hit show.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:45.280
Of course, Ethan Suplee was known for his work on&nbsp;
the big screen long before he became a TV star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.120
and he's still relatively active&nbsp;
in Hollywood today. In 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.720
Suplee appeared in Peter Berg's "Deepwater&nbsp;
Horizon'' alongside Mark Wahlberg and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:58.400
Kurt Russell, playing an oil rig worker&nbsp;
named Jason Anderson. A few years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:03.880
he played Gilbert Coney in Edward Nortion's&nbsp;
period private eye flick, "Motherless Brooklyn."

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:08.400
His most controversial feature came out&nbsp;
in 2020, as Suplee was one of those who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.480
appeared in the satirical political&nbsp;
thriller "The Hunt." Playing Gary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.960
the conspiracy theorist podcaster with a chip&nbsp;
on his shoulder, Suplee showed off his new look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:22.840
on the big screen, though not for long. Within&nbsp;
the movie's first week of release, the COVID-19&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:28.080
pandemic closed movie theaters everywhere,&nbsp;
forcing the Blumhouse production underground.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:33.520
Thankfully, the failure of "The Hunt" didn't&nbsp;
prevent Suplee from landing other roles. In 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.240
he appeared in "Dog" opposite Channing Tatum&nbsp;
and Damien Chazelle's Hollywood epic, "Babylon."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:40.000
The following year, he had three movies come out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.000
"Manodrome," "Blood for Dust," and the&nbsp;
action crime thriller "God Is a Bullet."

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.520
Since the beginning of Ethan Suplee's career,&nbsp;
the actor has collaborated with some pretty big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:56.360
names. After "Remember the Titans," he worked&nbsp;
with Denzel Washington again on "John Q." and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:01.280
"Unstoppable." Following "American History&nbsp;
X," he reunited with Edward Norton nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:06.920
two decades later for "Motherless Brooklyn."&nbsp;
Additionally, he and Ashton Kutcher have starred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:12.520
opposite one another a few times, including "The&nbsp;
Butterfly Effect," the comedy short "A Night of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:18.960
140 Tweets," and "The Ranch." But Suplee's most&nbsp;
frequent collaborator extends back to his roots.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:26.120
In 1995, Suplee landed his first feature film, a&nbsp;
small indie flick directed by Kevin Smith called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:31.280
"Mallrats." From then on, Suplee and Smith kept&nbsp;
working together, with the former appearing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:37.429
other Smith-directed productions: "Chasing&nbsp;
Amy," "Dogma," Clerks II," and "Clerks III."

00:05:37.429 --> 00:05:42.440
Amazingly, these two have also collaborated&nbsp;
on projects Smith didn't direct. They both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:48.040
appear in "Fanboys," "Drawing Flies," and&nbsp;
"Vulgar," the latter of which Smith executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.920
produced. Additionally, Smith produced&nbsp;
"A Better Place," which features Suplee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:57.760
and the two appeared in the same "A Night&nbsp;
of 140 Tweets" special Kutcher was in.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.560
Although it's more common to see Hollywood actors&nbsp;
play video game characters in movie or television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:07.680
adaptations, since the 2010s, it's become&nbsp;
increasingly popular to see actors transition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:14.440
from the filmmaking world into the video game&nbsp;
space. In 2022, Ethan Suplee continued that trend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:19.400
playing the mysterious hunter Bobby in&nbsp;
the interactive horror game "The Quarry."

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:23.640
One part "Friday the 13th," one part&nbsp;
"The Thing," and all parts frightening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:28.120
"The Quarry" is a slasher game that preys&nbsp;
upon your fears of the unknown and stars a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:32.600
pretty impressive cast. Suplee's role is&nbsp;
somewhat out-of-character for the actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.600
but that's part of what makes his&nbsp;
transition to video games so special.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:42.280
Beyond acting, Ethan Suplee has kept himself&nbsp;
fairly busy, and not just in the gym. Outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:47.680
of his fitness regimen, Suplee hosts a podcast,&nbsp;
"American Glutton," where he "sets out to examine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:53.600
our current obesity crisis." To do this, Suplee&nbsp;
is candid about his own battle with weight loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:58.440
and dietary health and often brings on&nbsp;
experts and others with similar stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:03.080
to help set the stage and encourage people&nbsp;
to get healthy. He even runs a regularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:07.707
updated blog through the podcast's website,&nbsp;
sharing the stories of many of his listeners.

00:07:07.707 --> 00:07:12.800
Naturally, "American Glutton" also features a&nbsp;
variety of celebrity guests, including those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:17.880
Suplee has previously worked with. With folks&nbsp;
like his "Unstoppable" co-star T.J. Miller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:21.760
former "My Name is Earl" guest&nbsp;
star Michael Peña, Kevin Smith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:26.400
and Josh Peck, Suplee does his best to&nbsp;
break any stigmas attached to weight loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.360
all the while encouraging those&nbsp;
who feel hopeless to push on.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:35.360
In an interview with Michael Rosenbaum on his&nbsp;
"Inside of You" podcast, Suplee shares that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.240
his producer, Kevin Connolly, wanted him&nbsp;
to host a politically-based podcast. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:43.480
uninterested in sharing his political&nbsp;
views in fear of being canceled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:48.160
he shifted the topic entirely, and from&nbsp;
there, "American Glutton" was born.

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:51.480
"One day I woke up when he was&nbsp;
asking me and I was like, ' I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:55.720
could fully talk about health and&nbsp;
fitness because I've done that.'"

00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:00.440
Despite the personal success Ethan Suplee&nbsp;
found in his physical transformation and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:05.760
podcast's impact on countless others, changing his&nbsp;
entire look hasn't been the easiest thing for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:11.720
career. Speaking about the series "Chance,"&nbsp;
he told Entertainment Weekly back in 2017 -

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:14.840
"Initially, the casting directors&nbsp;
didn't want me to come in because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:18.800
I'd lost a bunch of weight. Apparently&nbsp;
they had proposed me a bunch of times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.840
but the casting directors were&nbsp;
like, 'No, he's too thin now.'"

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:28.680
The actor notes that after "My Name is Earl,"&nbsp;
which ended in 2009, he got down to 9% body fat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:34.120
by continuously riding bicycles. But because of&nbsp;
his dramatic weight loss, nobody recognized him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:40.040
as the same person. It was then that Suplee took a&nbsp;
different approach, telling Entertainment Weekly -

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:44.800
"I'm not going to kill myself to be thin when&nbsp;
nobody knows me as a thin person. I do think it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:49.760
is affecting the kind of jobs I'm getting.&nbsp;
And so I just kind of relaxed on my diet."

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.320
But relaxing on his diet didn't mean that&nbsp;
Suplee was unhealthy. On the contrary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:59.360
the actor bulked up and is at a place where&nbsp;
he's happy with his weight and physique.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:04.840
Of course, Suplee's weight loss journey began when&nbsp;
he started dating his future wife, Brandy Lewis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:11.680
the younger sister of actor-singer Juliette&nbsp;
Lewis, in 2001. As Suplee told People in 2022 -

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:15.520
"I became, for the first time in my&nbsp;
life, kind of interested in the future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:19.680
and having experiences with her. Like&nbsp;
spending a day walking around a museum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.600
or going on a trip or hiking that I&nbsp;
just wasn't physically able to do."

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:29.720
Suplee went on to explain that while he had been&nbsp;
crazy about Lewis since the pair were teenagers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:33.720
it took her much longer to feel&nbsp;
the same way. Once she did however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:39.080
it was the first time in Suplee's life that he&nbsp;
felt accepted by someone for who he was as opposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:44.720
to what he looked like. From there, the couple&nbsp;
married in 2006 and they have four daughters.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:48.960
"I was possibly going to&nbsp;
have the life that I wanted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:52.800
but it required a little extra effort on my part."

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:56.920
The support of Ethan Suplee's family wasn't&nbsp;
the only thing that changed his perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:01.520
on physical health. In the very first&nbsp;
episode of his "American Glutton" podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:06.840
Suplee recounts a strange encounter he had with&nbsp;
"Passion of the Christ" star Jim Caviezel while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.720
on his way to film "Cold Mountain." On the&nbsp;
plane, Caviezel sat beside the actor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:17.200
confronted him on his way of life, specifically&nbsp;
noting, according to Suplee, that if your life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:22.920
doesn't emulate Jesus Christ, you're going to&nbsp;
hell. Suplee recalled the conversation, saying -

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:27.000
"It was the first conversation of that&nbsp;
nature that I'd ever had with somebody.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:31.920
Nobody had ever talked to me like that.&nbsp;
Nobody had ever said: 'Look at yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:37.560
you're going to f—ing hell... You don't&nbsp;
care about yourself at all, you're a mess.'"

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:42.880
Suplee stated that Caviezel was never unkind to&nbsp;
him during this encounter, but nevertheless, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:48.440
conversation understandably shook him. The actor&nbsp;
recalled his feelings after the talk, saying -

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:52.880
"I'm not saying he was right or that my&nbsp;
decision had anything to do with Jesus Christ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.400
but my decision was, 'I don't want people&nbsp;
to feel they can talk to me like that.'"

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:01.320
A breath of fresh air nowadays,&nbsp;
Ethan Suplee is hard to offend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:03.520
even after his incredible weight loss journey.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:07.480
"You're in these movies and you were very,&nbsp;
you know, overweight, does that - obese?"

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:11.160
"I was-you, whatever - You can't&nbsp;
offend me with those words."

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:14.760
For a long time, the actor was known&nbsp;
because of his overweight stature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:19.040
a look that helped him land roles in "Remember&nbsp;
the Titans" and "My Name is Earl," but that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:24.160
part of his life is behind him now, going&nbsp;
on to say on the "Timcast IRL" podcast -

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:28.480
"I've definitely had some people say&nbsp;
that, 'I preferred you fat,' and I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.120
don't care. […] I had been wanting to be&nbsp;
the size I am now for so long that like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:35.880
how you want me to be doesn't really matter."

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:39.000
Using his influence on social&nbsp;
media, through his podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:43.600
and his physical on-screen transformation,&nbsp;
Suplee hopes to inspire others to step up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:48.200
and make the change themselves. According&nbsp;
to Suplee, this doesn't necessarily require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:53.000
expunging foods from your diet but rather&nbsp;
a balance. He said on "Timcast IRL" -

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:55.440
"Taking an entire food group and demonizing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:58.960
and cutting it out of my&nbsp;
life, that's also extreme."

